## 3. The cited PDF
22:48:42Z  curl -sS -L https://lume.ufrgs.br/bitstream/handle/10183/243963/Resumo_72921.pdf (TLS verification on)
           -> HTTP 200, 111271 bytes, PDF 1.4, 2 pages, SHA-256 465cf2e30063b5528fc0f3af3db968f3beb251473866ae7664ae6ac841b6016d
Page 1: record of the event (Salão UFRGS 2021: SIC - XXXIII Salão de Iniciação Científica da UFRGS; 2021; held online;
        author Manuel Speranza Torres Veras; advisor Rodrigo Orsini Braga).
Page 2: the abstract itself, one page in Portuguese. Its content, summarised:
  - a computer search for integral unicyclic graphs with SageMath, starting from results of R. O. Braga et al. (2020);
  - it reports that C_{4,1}(16,32,16,0), with 68 vertices, is integral (= C_{4,1}(0,16,32,16) in the notation of BMS);
  - it reports the eigenvalues 0, +-sqrt(p), +-sqrt(2p+4) for the family C_{4,1}(p,2p,p,0), so that its members are
    integral when p and 2p+4 are both perfect squares;
  - it does NOT state the range of the search (no bound on the order, the cycle length or the parameters), and it
    names no other graph.

## 4. Overlap with Theorem 2 of the paper (no other integral generalized sun graph up to 41 vertices)
The integral members of C_{4,1}(0,a,2a,a), a >= 1, need a and 2a+4 to be squares (BMS prove "if and only if").
python3: [(a, 4a+4) for a < 100000 with a and 2a+4 squares] = [(16, 68), (576, 2308), (19600, 78404)].
So every graph named in the abstract has at least 68 vertices: nothing it reports overlaps Theorem 2 (or Theorem 1).
Because the range of the search is not stated, it may have covered part of the range of Theorem 2 without
reporting anything there. The paper says exactly this and cites the abstract next to Braga-Rodrigues-Trevisan.
